\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: SNB Action To Check CHF Gains Wouldn't Surprise\b0\par\par\uc2 1,408 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2010\par 11:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0904 GMT [Dow Jones] Any action by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  to prevent a decline in EUR/CHF wouldn't be surprising in the present circumstances, says Mizuho. "The franc is benefitting by default from the debt crisis in the EU, and I would be surprised if the SNB was \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to relieve the pressure on EUR/CHF," says Neil Jones, head of European hedge-fund sales at Mizuho. "The franc is attracting safe-haven flows, and the SNB will be well aware of this." EUR/CHF was at 1.4320 by 0900 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0901 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CZK trades higher at 25.598-25.628 from 25.549 late Tuesday as worries over the extent of some European countries to meet debt obligations weighs on central Europe's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "It's not a panic, but people are going into dollars and leaving Europe, it's a problem for emerging markets," a trader at \uc2 Komercni Banka\uc2  says. Despite the selling pressure, the koruna's losses are not as bad as in the rest of region and the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still seen as "a bit of safe haven in this environment", the trader adds. (sean.carney@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0858 GMT [Dow Jones] As long as the market remains concerned about the problems in Greece, EUR/CHF should remain under pressure, says \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . As long this is the case, a quick exit from SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains rather difficult. But since deflation risk in \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  is waning, bank believes the SNB will increasingly distance itself from actively impacting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the coming months. Meanwhile, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  expects SNB to begin to raise interest rates from their current extreme lows in the second half of the year. (goran.mijuk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0852 GMT [Dow Jones] The decision of \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  to downgrade Greek debt to 'junk' status could have serious consequences for European banks, says Geoffrey Yu at \uc2 UBS\uc2 . Says the risk now looms that banks will be unwilling to lend to their peers for fear the counter parties are excessively burdened with illiquid assets, not CDOs this time but Greek/Portuguese bonds. Says while we are not yet at the extremes, current market developments closely mirror the genesis of the funding crisis two years ago, and investors will need to be on their guard. (peter.nurse@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0845 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP higher late, underpinned by eurozone worries triggered by downgrades of Greece, Portugal; pair at three-week high 44.80 from 44.43 late Tuesday amid heavy trading. "The downgrades reflect worries on debt payments, which sparked risk aversion," says local bank trader. Pair tipped to advance further Thursday; next resistance 45.00, support 44.70. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0845 GMT [Dow Jones] Wednesday's March unemployment rate decrease, to 9.1% from 9.3% in February, implies that the Swedish labor market recovery is still on track, and even seem to surpass expectations, says Swedbank. Adds: "If this trend is to continue the Riksbank will have to revise its labor market forecasts to the better again." Sweden's two-year bond yield trades ten basis points lower at 1.29%, while the 10-year bond yield trades 12bps lower at 2.86%. (karl.bruze@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0843 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Bharti Airtel\uc2  (532454.BY) has declared worst may have passed in India's telecom price war but investors would be wise to ignore such proclamations, says Heard on the Street. Comments came after Bharti posted 8.2% drop in net profit for March-end quarter, due to stiff competition for its No.1 share of wireless market. Twelve operators, many of them relatively new companies, slashing prices in an effort to erode bigger rivals' market share: Bharti's share of mobile market dropped to 22.6% from 24.6% year ago, according to Telecom Regulatory Authority of India data. Price-war also eroding profitability -- mobile EBITDA margins dropped to 29.2% from 31.5% year before. If this tough on Bharti, harder on smaller rivals which lack Bharti's economies of scale. Bharti has proven able to find resources for expansion overseas, in Africa for example. For smaller players, mergers among themselves or being gobbled up by larger rivals is more likely outcome. That will be real sign this bloody war is over. See: "=HEARD ON THE STREET: India's Telecom Battle Isn't Over Yet." (harsh.joshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0838 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds higher; 8.20% 2022 at intraday high of 101.50 vs 100.82 Tuesday's close on demand from traders on view note could be closest proxy for new 10-year issuance; 7.02% 2016 bond, which highest traded in session, at 97.39 vs 97.28 last close. "There is generally a proxy bond that the market trades in along with the benchmark 10-year. The 2022 bond could take that place," says dealer with state-run bank. Notes, demand for note strong all through session unlike other notes that have oscillated sharply. Adds, demand for 10-year issue at auction Friday likely to be strong as supply of INR50 billion relatively small, will be lapped up by traders. Cutoff likely 7.90%-7.95%, tips dealer. (anant.kala@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0837 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR higher at 3.2170 vs 3.1850 late yesterday on risk aversion amid renewed euro zone debt worries; dealers say rise also fueled by USD short-covering by players that had earlier bet on MYR appreciating further. "Given the current sentiment, it looks like the (USD/MYR) has potential to go higher. A test of the 3.2500 level in the near-term would not be out of the question," dealer says; tips pair to move in 3.2000-3.2300 range tomorrow. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0835 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD turns sharply higher to 31.539 late, highest since April 13, from nearly whole session of losses (31.360 intraday low, 31.412 yesterday), as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of buying USD heavily late session to deter speculators, three traders at local bank say. Two traders estimate \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have bought US$300 million-US$400 million late session. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took advantage of an easing in the rising trend (of New Taiwan dollar)" to deter recent short-USD speculators, one says. Still, tips pair biased down in 31.450-31.550 band Thursday as market's short-USD bias remains intact. Volume heavy at US$1.6 billion, highest since Oct. 1, 2009, as some foreign investors' cover shorts while exporters sell for month-end settlements. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0834 GMT [Dow Jones] The new fund industry rules proposed by Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last night won't have much impact on Singapore's hedge fund industry, says Peter Douglas, counsel member for AIMA Asia Pacific. The MAS has "done a good job of balancing the huge advantages of (exempting some funds from regulatory oversight) with the need to be seen as tightening up, in line with global trends." Proposed rules include tightening capital requirements, compulsory licensing, and requiring asset managers to keep asset with a custodian. These rules will apply only to larger firms, which should have these in place already anyway, Douglas says; adds, "there is nothing even remotely onerous for any sensible medium-sized boutique." Under the proposed rules, funds with less than S$250 million of assets can remain unregulated. (ellen.sheng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0833 GMT Greece/10-year bund spread at 960bps, again pushing above historic record levels, but the number is iconic because of an absence of trades on the local HDAT bond trading platform. "There is some very light interbank trade, but I can't see anything changing hands on the HDAT," a senior local bond trader says. "At such pricing levels the market is factoring in a 40% haircut in the event of a debt restructuring, which seems more certain every day," he says. "This is an extreme phenomenon and yesterday's S&P downgrading Greece to junk status was just the icing on the cake," he adds. (nskrekas@trk.forthnet.gr) [ 04-28-10 0504ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100428e64s0006t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
