\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-SNB's Jordan says too early to raise rates-paper\b0\par\par\uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2010\par 18:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Vice-chairman says rates to be normalised eventually\par \par \uc2 * Swiss must honour U.S. tax agreement, regardless of \uc2 UBS\par \par \uc2 * Swiss banks would not be destabilised by Greek default\par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, background)\par \par \uc2 By Catherine Bosley\par \par \uc2 ZURICH, Feb 9 (Reuters) -The deputy chairman of the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  said on Tuesday it was too early to raise interest rates although prolonging a loose policy was not without dangers.\par \par \uc2 "It's still too early to exit zero monetary policy. The target for the three-month LIBOR remains at 0.25 percent and we will prevent an excessive appreciation of the franc so that exports aren't hurt," \uc2 Thomas Jordan\uc2  was quoted as saying by the newspaper Finanz und Wirtschaft.\par \par \uc2 "But monetary policy cannot remain this expansive for eternity. Our inflation forecast indicates that a normalisation of monetary policy will be necessary," he said. "We are aware that such a (loose) policy, especially over the longer term, is not without risks."\par \par \uc2 To combat the worst recession in decades and deflationary pressures, the SNB launched unconventional measures including rock-bottom rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  emerged from recession last summer, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has begun to unwind some of these measures.\par \par \uc2 Recent economic data have pointed to recovery and most economists expect a small interest rate increase in the second half of 2010. <0#FES:>\par \par \uc2 The SNB forecasts growth of 0.5 to 1.0 percent for 2010 and inflation of 0.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 At its Dec. 10 meeting, the SNB modified its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance, saying it would prevent an 'excessive' rise of the franc rather than any rise.\par \par \uc2 Since then the franc has risen steadily to hit a 15-month high against the euro last week before dropping back somewhat as markets assume the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the appreciation.\par \par \uc2 Jordan also said that a Greek default would not destabilise Swiss banks.\par \par \uc2 TAX DEAL\par \par \uc2 Jordan said pressures from Germany, France and the United States on the Alpine country's bank secrecy were not at the moment hurting financial stability.\par \par \uc2 "At the moment these disputes are not threatening the stability of the Swiss financial system. But we must find a solution so that the situation does not escalate," he said.\par \par \uc2 In August, Switzerland agreed to reveal the names of about 4,450 wealthy American clients of UBS AG , which had to take state aid during the financial crisis, to U.S authorities in a tax dispute settlement. [ID:nLJ718259]\par \par \uc2 "Switzerland is required to honour the agreement signed with the U.S., independent of the situation with \uc2 UBS\uc2 ," he said. "In the longer term everything must be done, so that single companies don't bring the state into such a precarious situation again."\par \par \uc2 Jordan also said that thanks to positive market developments, the SNB was more likely to be reimbursed for the \uc2 UBS\uc2  toxic asset fund.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Ron Askew)\par \par \uc2 SWISS-SNB/JORDAN (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100209e629001k7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
