\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MIDDAY BRIEFING FROM DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\b0\par\par\uc2 1,123 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2010\par 13:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 AMERICAN MARKETS OUTLOOK: U.S. stock futures indicate a slightly lower open Friday as investors wait for the ever-important payroll numbers.\par \par \uc2 "Futures are not down as much as they could be considering the problems with Greece and China," said James Hughes, market analyst at CMC Markets in London. "Investors are waiting for the employment figures, and the ADP numbers earlier in the week suggest that they might be encouraging."\par \par \uc2 European stocks and the euro have again been hit hard as investors' worries grow over the more heavily indebted economies in the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 At 1131 GMT, S&P 500 futures were down 7.8 points at 1053.90, Nasdaq 100 futures were down 5.50 points at 1729.25 and Dow industrial futures were down 63 points at 9916.00.\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN MARKETS: European markets are sharply lower around midday as fears grow over the debt levels in the euro zone's "peripheral" nations escalate and may spread to other European economies. "Greek, Spanish and Portuguese spreads are widening further over bunds this morning, fueling speculation that only direct EU \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can bring the crisis to a halt," says \uc2 Lloyds Banking Group\uc2 . Traders now look to U.S. nonfarm payrolls data at 1330 GMT.\par \par \uc2 London's FTSE 100 is down 1.79% at 5047.32, amid mounting concerns over debt in Greece, Spain and Portugal. Miners post big losses as metals prices slide, with Vedanta down 5.3% and \uc2 Lonmin\uc2  down 4.8%. "This downward momentum is likely to be exaggerated by the fact the Dow is now forecast to open below that psychologically significant 10,000 level, and with volatility only likely to increase further after the release of today's nonfarm payrolls, the risk of further selling cannot be underestimated," said Anthony Grech at \uc2 IG Index\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Frankfurt the DAX is down 1.5% at 5448.37. In Paris the CAC 40 is down 2.5% at 3598.70.\par \par \uc2 Bunds and gilts are higher as risk aversion builds. At 1129 GMT, March bunds were up 0.42 at 124.12, while March gilts were up 0.30 at 116.15.\par \par \uc2 Global aversion to risk, tumbling equity prices and new record highs for the price of credit default swaps helped to keep the dollar rising in Europe. At 1129 GMT, the dollar was up at Y89.50, the euro was down at $1.371, off a fresh eight-month low of $1.3648, and the pound was down at $1.5710, also off a fresh eight-month low of $1.5655.\par \par \uc2 ===========================\par \par \uc2 TOP STORIES:\par \par \uc2 BRITISH AIRWAYS\uc2  IMPROVES BUT FACES RECORD LOSSES: \uc2 British Airways\uc2  says 3Q pretax losses narrow to GBP50 million from GBP122 million in the same period a year ago, due to a decline in operating costs, but warns it's still on course for its worst fiscal-year performance.\par \par \uc2 VOLVO REPORTS NET LOSS BUT SEES SIGNS OF RECOVERY: Volvo reports a fifth consecutive quarterly net loss but sees signs of recovery in some markets and posts a rise in orders. Net loss widens to SEK2 billion missing forecasts of a SEK856 million loss.\par \par \uc2 CREDIT SUISSE SHARES FALL OVER BANKING SECRECY WORRIES: Shares in \uc2 Credit Suisse Group\uc2  on fears that its private bank will be hit by wealthy clients withdrawing funds because of growing pressure on Swiss banking secrecy.\par \par \uc2 ============================\par \par \uc2 INSIGHT & ANALYSIS FROM DOW JONES NEWSWIRES:\par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  FOCUS: For sterling, policy may be one problem but it is politics that could do the real damage to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 =BEAT FOCUS: Those who think current concerns over European sovereign borrowers are overdone should take a look at the relative performance of Europe's financial and corporate issuers.\par \par \uc2 =MONEY TALKS: Like a dodgy Toyota, markets for the past eight months have had their accelerator stuck flat to the floor. Speed junkies loved it. But then the tree got in the way.\par \par \uc2 ===========================\par \par \uc2 STILL TO COME\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ET/GMT   COUNTRY/PERIOD \par 0700/1200  CAN  Jan     Employment Report \par 0830/1330  US   Jan     Employment Report \par 0830/1330  CAN  Dec     Stocks Of Cdn Grain At Dec. 31 \par 1100/1600  US           Treasury Chief Economist Krueger briefs on unemployment \par                         data and economic recovery in Washington \par 1500/2000  US   Dec     Consumer Credit \par 1720/2220  US           St. Louis Fed Pres Bullard speaks in St. Louis \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ===========================\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OTHER NEWS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA NAMES BANKS IN EUROBOND ISSUANCE: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  and VTB Capital are hired by Russia's finance ministry to organize the country's first Eurobond sale in a decade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UK PPI RISES AT FASTEST PACE FOR 13 MONTHS: Rising gasoline prices lifted U.K. factory-gate prices at their fastest pace for 13 months in January, marking their eleventh straight rise, although core inflation eased, the Office for National Statistics says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GERMAN INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT FALLS UNEXPECTEDLY: German industrial production fell unexpectedly in December from a month earlier, as weak orders sapped manufacturers' momentum at the end of the year, the economics ministry said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ICAP SEES LOWER PRETAX PROFIT: U.K.-based inter-dealer broker ICAP says that fiscal 3Q revenue was "slightly ahead" of the same period last year, but lowers it full-year pretax profit guidance as investments in new businesses haven't reaped profits yet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BG GROUP PROFIT SLUMPS 39% ON WEAKER PRICES: \f28 \uc2 \f2 BG Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  posts a 38.5% fall in net profit for 4Q due to weaker natural gas prices. Profit for the three months ended December 31 2009 totals GBP465 million, compared with GBP756 million for 4Q of 2008.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LIBERTY INTERNATIONAL MULLS SALE OF U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 OPERATIONS\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : Liberty International, the U.K.'s largest retail real estate investment trust, is considering options for its U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  including a potential disposal, but will keep its partnership with rival REIT \f28 \uc2 \f2 Great Portland Estates\f28 \uc2 \f2 , people close to the situation say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OECD SHOWS 'STRONGER SIGNALS' OF RECOVERY: There are increasingly strong signals that the world economy is in recovery, with leading developed economies clearly set to expand, the Organisation for Economic Co-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Development says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HERMES SALES RISE 8.5%, UPS EARNINGS TARGET: French luxury goods company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hermes International\f28 \uc2 \f2  reports a higher-than-expected 8.5% rise in 2009 revenues and raises expectations for full-year earnings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BMW CHINA SALES MORE THAN DOUBLE: \f28 \uc2 \f2 BMW\f28 \uc2 \f2  says that sales in China more than double on the year last month , the latest sign that the dynamic Chinese market is fueling the recovery in the luxury car segment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWISS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK INTERVENES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AS FRANC PLUNGES: The Swiss franc falls to multi-month lows against the euro and dollar in Asia as market participants said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Switzerland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  makes a rare and aggressive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Katrina Bishop, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 207 842 9376; katrina.bishop@dowjones.com [ 05-02-10 1205GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 70737   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100205e625001lq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
