\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Falls As Concern Eases Over Dubai's Debt\b0\par\par\uc2 By Fabio Alves   \par \uc2 OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 729 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 2009\par 22:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar tumbled against most rivals Tuesday as concerns over Dubai's debt situation receded, spurring investors to snap up global stocks, commodities and higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Easing fears about Dubai's debt crisis spreading to other credit markets "is helping to relieve some concerns that had weighed on riskier assets," said Omer Esiner, senior market analyst at Travelex Global Business Payments in Washington.\par \par \uc2 The greenback remained under pressure versus other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  except the yen, which fell across the board on the announcement of a surprise meeting by the Bank of Japan.\par \par \uc2 Late afternoon Tuesday in New York, the euro was at $1.5086, from $1.5015 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y86.68, from Y86.45, while the euro was at Y130.76, from Y129.80. The U.K. pound was at $1.6617, from $1.6456. The dollar was at CHF0.9994, from CHF1.0043.\par \par \uc2 The Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of six \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 74.426, from 74.788.\par \par \uc2 "At least for now the market is under the impression that the Dubai situation is more or less contained," Esiner said.\par \par \uc2 Safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have therefore lost their appeal and investors have reverted to the previous trend of selling the dollar and buying commodities, stocks and emerging market assets, he said.\par \par \uc2 However, investor caution ahead of further details on the Dubai situation and Friday's key U.S. non-farm payrolls report should keep the dollar's losses in check, said John McCarthy, manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at ING Capital Markets in New York.\par \par \uc2 To see the dollar moves against the yen, please see chart at:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/3118"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/3118 }}}\par \par \uc2 State-run conglomerate \uc2 Dubai World\uc2  broke six days of silence early Tuesday morning and announced details of its proposed debt restructuring, saying it was having constructive talks with banks over some $26 billion in affected debt.\par \par \uc2 That bolstered investor appetite for risk in global markets overnight, fueling a rally in oil, metals and stocks.\par \par \uc2 A weaker-than-expected report on U.S. manufacturing activity failed to damp investor sentiment. The Institute for Supply Management's November manufacturing index came in at 53.6, trailing economists' estimates for reading of 55.0, and less than the 55.7 reported in October.\par \par \uc2 Still, the index clocked in above the 50 threshold, indicating manufacturing activity remained in expansion territory.\par \par \uc2 Though the dollar declined against most of its rivals, it gained ground against the yen after a surprise overnight meeting of the BOJ. The dollar rose as far as Y87.54 on the news of the emergency meeting, but gave up most of those gains when the BOJ didn't announce specific measures to arrest the appreciation of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Instead, BOJ board members agreed to provide more liquidity to markets by lending up to Y10 trillion in three-month funds at the 0.1% policy rate. That was seen largely as a political gesture rather than a shift in policy.\par \par \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight bucked the ultra-loose monetary policy trend by announcing an expected increase in key interest rates by 0.25 percentage point, putting its key rate at 3.75%. Because analysts had expected the increase, the Australian dollar had already appreciated ahead of the announcement and actually lost some ground. It has since gained on the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  said overnight it would continue to act decisively to prevent the Swiss franc from strengthening against the euro, SNB President \uc2 Jean-Pierre Roth\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The SNB first announced its strategy of acting against Swiss franc strength in the spring in an attempt to support \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-driven economy, and is thought to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets four times since then.\par \par \uc2 Several times in recent months the franc has appreciated to levels against the dollar and euro greater than what the SNB is thought to tolerate, but the SNB has not always \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , keeping investors guessing.\par \par \uc2 -By Fabio Alves, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2204;\par \par \uc2 fabio.alves@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Bradley Davis in New York contributed to this article.) [ 12-01-09 1645ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091201e5c1000ey\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
