\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Update\b0\par\par\uc2 1,314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2009\par 01:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 US Dollar\par \par \uc2 The dollar received a boost against the euro and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday as investors were less sanguine about the pace of economic recovery and U.S stocks snapped a six-session winning streak. Caution overtook optimism regarding a speedy exit from the global slump and risk-sensitive assets yielded ground after strong advances earlier in the week. Late afternoon, the euro was at $1.4841 from $1.4976 late Wednesday. The dollar was at Y90.40 from Y89.82.\par \par \uc2 Euro\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD to trade with bearish bias; spotlight on 1000 GMT euro-zone 3Q flash estimate GDP (expected +0.6% on quarter, minus 3.9% on year). EUR/USD undermined by unwinding of long-EUR carry trades on higher investor risk aversion, weaker-than-expected 0.3% rise in euro-zone September industrial production (vs forecast for 0.5% increase), official concern over EUR strength. But EUR/USD downside limited by EUR-USD interest differential, sovereign rebalancing of reserves in favor of EUR, position adjustments before weekend. Other data focus: 0700 GMT German 3Q GDP (1st release). EUR/USD daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics turned bearish. Support at 1.4819 (yesterday''''s low), then at 1.4807 (Nov. 5 low); breach would expose downside to 1.4716 (base of ascending channel formed with July 1 high of 1.4201 and Aug. 17 low of 1.4044, coinciding with 55-day moving average), then 1.4623 (Nov. 3 reaction low). Resistance at 1.4943 (hourly chart); breach would expose upside to 1.5016 (yesterday''''s high), then 1.5048 (Wednesday''''s high), 1.5061 (Oct. 26 peak) and 1.5163 (76.4% Fibonacci retracement of decline from July 15, 2008 top of 1.6039 to Oct. 28, 2008 bottom of 1.2328).\par \par \uc2 Japanese Yen\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY to consolidate amid broadly stronger USD undertone as weaker Wall Street triggers flows to safe-haven USD. U.S. stocks retreated despite bigger-than-expected 12,000 drop in latest U.S. weekly jobless claims to 11-month low of 502,000 (vs forecast for just 2,000 decrease) as investors hesitant to make new bets after Wall Street''''s 6-day winning run. USD/JPY also underpinned by USD demand for import settlements; but topside limited by unwinding of JPY-funded carry trades amid increased risk aversion, Japan exporter sales, lower U.S. Treasury yields, loose U.S. monetary policy, position adjustments before weekend. Data focus: 0430 GMT Japan September revised industrial production & retail sales, 1330 GMT U.S. September trade balance, 1455 GMT U.S. November University Of Michigan consumer confidence index (preliminary). USD/JPY daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics turned bullish at oversold. Resistance at 90.61 (yesterday''''s high), then at 90.85 (Nov. 5 and 6 highs); breach would expose upside to 91.32 (Nov. 4 reaction high), then 91.62 (Oct. 29 high) and 92.32 (Oct. 27 reaction high). Support at 89.62 (yesterday''''s low); breach would target 89.26 (Wednesday''''s low), then 89.17 (Nov. 2 reaction low), 88.81 (Oct. 14 reaction low) and 88.00 (Oct. 7 trough).\par \par \uc2 Sterling\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD to trade with risks skewed lower. GBP/USD undermined by inflamed investor risk aversion, recent Fitch warning on UK triple-A sovereign credit ratings, official nod for weaker GBP, concerns over high UK public debt. But GBP/USD losses tempered by position adjustments before weekend. GBP/USD daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics falling from overbought. Support at 1.6514 (yesterday's low); breach would target 1.6465 (Nov. 5 low), then 1.6399 (Nov. 4 low), 1.6260 (Nov. 3 reaction low) and 1.6120 (previous cap set Oct. 8). Resistance at 1.6627 (yesterday's high); breach would target 1.6675 (hourly chart), then 1.6797 (Wednesday's high), 1.6843 (Monday's high) and 1.7042 (Aug. 5 peak).\par \par \uc2 Swiss Franc\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF to trade with positive bias, underpinned by broadly stronger USD undertone, fears of SNB's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; ZEW-\uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  survey of economic expectations in \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  showing its headline balance fell to 56.4 in November from October's 65, suggesting Swiss recovery may lose some of its vigor in near term. But USD/CHF topside limited by unwinding of short-CHF carry trades on growing investor risk aversion, position adjustments before weekend. USD/CHF daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics turned bullish. Resistance at 1.0193 (overnight high), then at 1.0201 (Nov. 6 high); breach would expose upside to 1.0277 (Nov. 4 high), then 1.0283 (55-day moving average) and 1.0338 (Nov. 3 reaction high). Support at 1.0108 (hourly chart); breach would expose downside to 1.0055 (yesterday's low), then 1.0029 (Oct. 23 trough), 1.0009 (July 15, 2008 reaction low) and parity.\par \par \uc2 Canadian Dollar\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD to trade with risks skewed higher. USD/CAD supported by stronger global USD, softer commodity and oil prices, mounting investor risk aversion, dovish BOC monetary stance, official concern over CAD strength. But USD/CAD gains tempered by bigger-than-expected 0.5% increase in Canada September new house prices (vs +0.2% forecast), position adjustments before weekend. USD/CAD daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics turning bullish near oversold; bullish key-reversal-day pattern completed yesterday. Resistance at 1.0577 (overnight high); breach would target 1.0608 (Tuesday's high), then 1.0746 (Monday's high), 1.0780 (Nov. 6 high) and 1.0853 (Nov. 3 high). Support at 1.0477 (hourly chart), then at 1.0413 (Oct. 22 & yesterday's lows); breach would reinstate near-term negative bias, targeting 1.0376 (Oct. 21 low), then 1.0264 (Oct. 20 reaction low) and 1.0204 (Oct. 15 trough).\par \par \uc2 Australian Dollar\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD to trade with risks skewed lower after retreating sharply from fresh 15-month high of 0.9369 yesterday. AUD/USD undermined by unwinding of long-AUD carry trades on stronger investor risk aversion, softer commodity prices. But AUD/USD downside limited by Aussie-U.S. yield gap, lingering effect from yesterday's upbeat Aussie October jobs data, position adjustments before weekend. AUD/USD daily chart negative-biased as stochastics turned bearish at overbought; bearish key-reversal-day pattern completed yesterday. Support at 0.9208, then at 0.9193; breach would expose downside to 0.9089, then 0.9022 and 0.9003. Resistance at 0.9310, then at 0.9369; breach would reinstate near-term positive bias, exposing upside to psychological 0.9400.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand Dollar\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD to trade lower, undermined by unwinding of long-NZD carry trades on heightened investor risk aversion, official concern about strong Kiwi, dovish RBNZ monetary stance. But NZD/USD downside limited by Kiwi-U.S. yield advantage, position adjustments before weekend. NZD/USD daily chart negative-biased as MACD in bearish mode, stochastics turning bearish. Support at 0.7304; breach would expose downside to 0.7219, then 0.7190, 0.7153 and 0.7078. Resistance at 0.7375; breach would expose upside to 0.7440, then 0.7461, 0.7515 and 0.7564.\par \par \uc2 China Yuan\par \par \uc2 Changes to the wording of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's third-quarter monetary policy report led to market talk that Beijing may soon allow the yuan to rise against the dollar, but that wasn't enough to boost the yuan, which inched lower against the dollar Thursday afternoon and hovered around the stable central parity rate. On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.8267 around 0730 GMT, up from Wednesday's close of CNY6.8259. It traded between CNY6.8263 and CNY6.8268. The dollar-yuan central parity rate was set at 6.8268, largely unchanged from 6.8267 Wednesday. It was set at 6.8268 Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2009 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020091113e5bd00001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
