\par\par \b \uc2 FUND VIEW-Correction in stocks to boost dollar-Capricorn\b0\par\par\uc2 By Wanfeng Zhou   \par \uc2 648 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 2009\par 19:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Aug 28 (Reuters) - The U.S. equity market is due for a pullback and this could support the dollar as an inverse correlation, in which the two assets tend to move in opposite directions, is intact, a Switzerland-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager said.\par \par \uc2 Over the past few months, the dollar has tended to rise when stocks retreat as investors see the greenback as a safe store of value in times of market stress. The link, although somewhat weaker, is set to stay for some time.\par \par \uc2 "We're pretty bullish on the dollar over the medium-term," said Mikkel Thorup, chief investment officer of Capricorn, which manages assets of about $280 million.\par \par \uc2 "We believe the equity market in general is over-stretched and there will be a correction very soon," he said. "There's not an awful lot of buying going on. There's still a lot of money on the sidelines that's not willing to actually go in and buy at these current levels."\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 has rallied more than 50 percent since it hit a 12-year low in early March as the United States appears to be emerging from its worst recession since the 1930s. There are concerns that the rally is overdone and that stock prices may have run ahead of fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 The recent sell-off in China's Shanghai composite <.SSEC> has reminded investors of significant risks and uncertainty facing the global economy and financial markets, Thorup said.\par \par \uc2 "Everybody is talking about China in terms of growth," Thorup said. "We've seen some pretty steep corrections already in China. (That) should definitely have an influence on the U.S. equity market."\par \par \uc2 He expects the dollar to climb to around $1.33 and $1.34 per euro by the end of the year. On Friday, the euro <EUR=> last traded at $1.4337, little changed on the day.\par \par \uc2 While the global economy may have hit bottom, the recovery is likely to be slow as banks are still reluctant to lend, consumer spending remains weak, and unemployment stays near 10 percent in the United States, Thorup said.\par \par \uc2 "Just in March, everybody was talking about a doomsday scenario. Then five months after, everything is really looking fantastic. That's what I just don't understand," he said.\par \par \uc2 "I think we're going to have a prolonged period where we'll actually see the markets (and the economy) moving not too far away from the bottom. I think we can call that an L-shape (recovery)."\par \par \uc2 STABLE SWISS FRANC\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager expects the Swiss franc to be "relatively stable" against both the dollar and euro, noting that the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is "very strong on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  side."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's solid fundamentals have supported the franc, but the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has limited the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's upside. Earlier this week, \uc2 SNB board member Thomas Jordan\uc2  said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not accept a further appreciation against the euro. See [ID:nWEA8258].\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the euro slipped 0.2 percent to 1.5155 francs <EURCHF=>, while the dollar fell 0.2 percent to 1.0568 <CHF=>.\par \par \uc2 But Thorup said uncertainty surrounding the U.S. tax settlement with \uc2 UBS\uc2  will likely put pressure on the Swiss economy.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland earlier this month agreed to reveal the names of about 4,450 wealthy American clients of \uc2 UBS\uc2  to U.S. authorities, creating uncertainty about the ramifications for other banks. See [ID:nLJ718259]\par \par \uc2 "We've seen several of the biggest players in the Swiss market beginning to outsource or offshore a lot of their (wealth) management business and of course that's having an impact also on the Swiss economy."\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Chizu Nomiyama)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /DOLLAR (FUND VIEW)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|J|Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090828e58s001d3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
