\par\par \b \uc2 SCENARIOS-What will the SNB decide at its policy meeting?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sven Egenter   \par \uc2 752 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 2009\par 15:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 ZURICH, June 15 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  looks set to stick to its ultra-loose monetary policy course at its next policy meeting on June 18, keeping up the fight against the worst recession in decades and the risk of deflation.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss economy shrank 0.8 percent on the quarter in the first three months of this year, its third quarterly drop in a row as global demand for Swiss products has collapsed and higher unemployment is slowing consumption.\par \par \uc2 Consumer prices fell one percent on the year in May, the steepest price decline since 1959.\par \par \uc2 "Against this background for Swiss growth, coupled with the threat of a deflationary spiral, it is clear that the SNB will be in no position to reverse their ultra-loose monetary policy stance in 2009," said 4Cast analyst Saara Tuuli.\par \par \uc2 Following are key scenarios for the outcome of the SNB's meeting:\par \par \uc2 MORE OF THE SAME\par \par \uc2 The Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is most likely to stick to its set of drastic unconventional easing measures adopted at the March 12 meeting as the recession has deepened and the price declines highlight the risks of a deflationary spiral in Switzerland.\par \par \uc2 In a Reuters poll, all 41 economists expect unchanged rates and most bet on continuing unorthodox easing.[ID:nLAG003501]\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered its target rate for 3-month Swiss franc LIBOR to 0.25 percent -- a level only seen in the 2003 recession until now -- as it forecast a 2.5-3.0 percent drop in gross domestic product 2009, the worst since 1975.\par \par \uc2 The SNB offered longer-term repos to bring LIBOR down.\par \par \uc2 It also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem a rise in the franc against the euro and started to buy corporate bonds in Swiss francs -- mostly covered bonds or Pfandbriefe.[ID:nLE924029]\par \par \uc2 SNB board member Thomas Jordan said in May the measures were showing the desired effects: credit spreads have narrowed, the franc is hovering in a range of 1.50-1.52 per euro and volatility has come down significantly.\par \par \uc2 Analysts hope for more details on the exact scope and size of the bond purchases and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Click on [ID:nL2504822] for a Q+A on the SNB's unorthodox measures.\par \par \uc2 STEPPING UP \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTIONS\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Some economists have noted that monetary conditions in \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  have not really eased over recent months despite the SNB's drastic actions. The franc remains strong against a basket of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trading partners.\par \par \uc2 The SNB has called \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  its strongest instrument to fight deflation, and it may want to weaken the franc more actively to provide some relief for Swiss exporters.\par \par \uc2 However, this would contradict the SNB official's repeated pledge that they would not engage in a devaluation race. Therefore, a more activist \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy seems unlikely.\par \par \uc2 NEW MEASURES\par \par \uc2 Right after the March decision, board member Jordan emphasised the SNB had more aces up its sleeve if needed:\par \par \uc2 * The SNB could add riskier assets to its list of collateral accepted in the repo and more counterparties could be allowed to take part.\par \par \uc2 * The SNB could impose negative interest rates on banks' deposits with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and in its liquidity \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * On the credit market, the SNB could buy bonds directly from the issuers or buy government bonds.\par \par \uc2 * As a final step on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  front, the SNB could set a fixed exchange rate target and defend it with unlimited purchases of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * The SNB may buy government bonds.\par \par \uc2 However, SNB officials have said patience is needed to allow the measures already taken to work.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts see a small chance of government bond purchases as yields have been rising.\par \par \uc2 TAKING BACK MEASURES\par \par \uc2 Taking back any of the unconventional easing measures is by far the least likely scenario, despite some more optimistic news on the global economy and first tepid signs that the economic slump maybe slowing in Switzerland, too.\par \par \uc2 The SNB officials said that a timely unwinding of the loose monetary policy was important, but they have given no indication that this would happen soon. "The inflation forecast will be the key instrument in determining when it is time to begin the exit strategy," Vice-Chairman Philipp Hildebrand said recently.\par \par \uc2 It is highly unlikely, however, that the SNB will sharply revise up its inflation forecast, which sees nearly unchanged prices in 2010 and 2011.\par \par \uc2 SWISS-SNB/ (SCENARIOS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090615e56f001gb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
