\par\par \b \uc2 SNB declines comment on franc drop\b0\par\par\uc2 189 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 2009\par 18:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 ZURICH, May 15 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  declined comment on Friday after market talk the Bank of International Settlements was in the market buying euros versus the Swiss franc on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "I do not comment on this," SNB spokesman Werner Abegg said when asked if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was behind the drop of the franc against the euro.\par \par \uc2 The euro rose to session highs at 1.5140 francs, but later came off to 1.5101, still up 0.3 percent on the day.\par \par \uc2 SNB officials have repeatedly warned that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wanted to fight a rise in the Swiss franc "resolutely" to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of deflation.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the franc on March 12, when it announced a set of drastic steps to boost the economy and fend off deflation.\par \par \uc2 Two traders said the BIS was definitely in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market buying the euro against the Swiss franc, but a BIS spokeswoman declined to comment. (Reporting by Sven Egenter; Editing by Andy Bruce)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /SNB (URGENT)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090515e55f002r7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
