\par\par \b \uc2 Swiss economy will hit bottom by '10-new SNB member\b0\par\par\uc2 399 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 2009\par 11:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 ZURICH, April 9 (Reuters) - The Swiss economy may have hit the bottom of the crisis by January 2010, Jean-Pierre Danthine, who will join the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  executive board at the start of next year, told a Swiss newspaper.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether he expects the worst of the crisis to be over by the time he takes up his new post in January, Danthine was quoted by Le Temps on Thursday as saying: "We could have seen the bottom, with the exception of unemployment."\par \par \uc2 "We will at that point make sure that the conditions for a recovery are the best possible, and that the biggest risk is inflation rather than deflation," he added.\par \par \uc2 The SNB has warned against the risk of deflation in \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  and said it was ready to use \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a way to defuse this threat.\par \par \uc2 Danthine, an economics professor at the University of Lausanne who also heads the Swiss Finance Institute, said he hoped the risk posed by Switzerland's largest bank \uc2 UBS\uc2  was over.\par \par \uc2 "We can now hope this (the risk) is now behind us," he said. "We are exploring several avenues -- capital requirements and the leverage ratio -- to avert this situation in the future."\par \par \uc2 He said he was also interested in the concept of "contingency capital", whereby institutions considered to pose systemic risks could get access to capital in specific situations.\par \par \uc2 "This can be a sort of insurance that would allow, in certain specific situations, an automatic injection of capital that could come from abroad," Danthine said. "The markets in their own entirety would contribute to this form of systemic insurance."\par \par \uc2 Danthine, whose appointment was announced on Wednesday, also appeared reluctant to support any heavy regulation of executive pay within the financial sector and said this was not a key responsibility for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Remuneration has played a certain role in the crisis, but we should not exaggerate," he said. "There is a need for improvement and if this is not done quickly enough, we may have to regulate."\par \par \uc2 "I would like to mention that socially responsible investment is a good way to introduce progress, possibly more effective than some big regulation, even if this does not grab newspaper headlines."\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Lisa Jucca; editing by David Stamp)\par \par \uc2 SWISS-SNB/|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090409e549000nk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
