\par\par \b \uc2 Moody's\uc2  issues annual sovereign credit report on Switzerland\b0\par\par\uc2 1,730 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Februar 2009\par \uc2 Moody's Investors Service Press Release\par \uc2 MOODPR\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009   \par \par \uc2 Switzerland's Aaa ratings are underpinned by very high levels of economic, institutional and financial strength, which contribute to low susceptibility to event risk, says Moody's Investors Service in its new annual sovereign credit report on Switzerland. The country's ratings have a stable outlook.\par \par \uc2 "Switzerland has high average incomes that are expected to be maintained over the long term, as well as an open, highly developed and diversified economy," says Alexander Kockerbeck, a Vice President-Senior Credit Officer in \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Sovereigns Group. "Furthermore, Switzerland has a long history of fiscal prudence, low inflation and a strong net external creditor position."\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  also notes that Switzerland's institutions are very robust and its governance system is highly transparent. This is supported by the country's political stability, although its referendum democracy can lend itself to a cumbersome reform and deregulation process. Furthermore, there is broad consensus on fiscal discipline and on the general goal to achieve balanced federal budgets.\par \par \uc2 "The small and very open Swiss economy will face recession - like many other European economies - due to the global financial and economic crisis," explains Mr. Kockerbeck. "The high losses among large Swiss banks and ongoing problems in the global financial markets have increased pressure on output and employment within Switzerland's important banking and insurance sector. However, the challenge should be manageable with no substantial macro imbalances or structural problems in the rest of the economy."\par \par \uc2 Although Switzerland's economic, financial and political characteristics have a track record of high solidity \uc2 Moody's\uc2  considers the country's susceptibility to event risk as "low" rather than "very low" due to the risks in the banking sector.\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  recognises that the Swiss government has recently extended support to its banking system during the ongoing financial crisis. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  considers the potential net impact on the government's balance sheet to be muted at this stage, since potential government capital injection into banks is counterbalanced by bank assets, and potential government guarantees for the banking sector represent (explicit) contingent liabilities.\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  also notes that the government takes calculated risks with the banking system, but still displays a strong crisis management capacity. The Swiss government's key strategy in combating the current financial crisis appears to be timely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and further crisis prevention.\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  believes that \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Aaa ratings are able to withstand a large-scale government support \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the Swiss banking system due to: (i) the underlying economic and institutional strength and financial robustness of the Swiss government; (ii) the quality of any bank assets that it would acquire through these support \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; and (iii) the likelihood that such an interposition of the government's balance sheet would be a better alternative to the potential damage to Switzerland's economic vitality.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, the government has a variety of options in case of an emergency. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  recognises the Swiss government's ability to raise potentially larger amounts of debt through: (i) its access to a large pool of domestic savings, as Switzerland has a savings rate equivalent to nearly one-third of GDP; (ii) the Swiss franc's role as an international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , supporting the country's excellent reputation; and (iii) for short-term liquidity pressures, the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unlimited swap line with the US Federal Reserve, which provides very precious relief at times when dollar funding is scarce.\par \par \uc2 Finally, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  does not rule out international support cooperation to prevent shock waves across the global financial system that a massive negative shock from the large Swiss banks may trigger, given their very international nature as highly integrated heavyweights in the global financial infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 The last rating action with respect to the Government of Switzerland was in November 1998 when the Aaa rating was assigned to the Swiss local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds.\par \par \uc2 The principal methodology used in rating the Government of Switzerland is \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Sovereign Bond Methodology, which can be found at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.moodys.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.moodys.com}}}\uc2  in the Credit Policy & Methodologies directory, in the Ratings Methodologies subdirectory. Other methodologies and factors that may have been considered in the process of rating this issuer can also be found in the Credit Policy & Methodologies directory on \uc2 Moody's\uc2  website.\par \par \uc2 The issuance of this credit report by Moody's Investors Service is an annual update to the markets and is not a formal action to alter the credit rating of the issuer.\par \par \uc2 * * * * *\par \par \uc2 NOTE TO JOURNALISTS ONLY: For a copy of this report, please contact EMEA Press Information in London +44-20-7772-5456; New York Press Information +1-212-553-0376; Juan Pablo Soriano in Madrid +34-91-310-1454; Alex Cataldo in Milan +39-02-914-81-100; Eric de Bodard in Paris +331-5330-1076; Detlef Scholz in Frankfurt +49-69-707-30-700; Mardig Haladjian in Limassol +357-25-586-586; Alex Sazhin in Moscow +7-495-228-60-60; Petr Vins in Prague +4202 2422 2929; Tokyo Press Information +813-5408-4110; Hilary Parkes in Toronto +1-416-214-1635; Hong Kong Press Information +852-2916-1150; Hector Lim in Sydney +612 9270 8102; Luiz Tess in S\u227\'00\'E3o Paulo +5511-3043-7300; Alberto Jones Tamayo in Mexico City +5255-1253-5700; Daniel R\u250\'00\'FAas in Buenos Aires +54 11-4816-2332 ext. 105; Craig Jamieson in Johannesburg +27-11-217-5470; Jehad el-Nakla in Dubai +971 4 401 9536; or visit our web site at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.moodys.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.moodys.com}}}\par \par \uc2 CREDIT RATINGS ARE MIS'S CURRENT OPINIONS OF THE RELATIVE FUTURE CREDIT RISK OF ENTITIES, CREDIT COMMITMENTS, OR DEBT OR DEBT-LIKE SECURITIES. MIS DEFINES CREDIT RISK AS THE RISK THAT AN ENTITY MAY NOT MEET ITS CONTRACTUAL, FINANCIAL OBLIGATIONS AS THEY COME DUE AND ANY ESTIMATED FINANCIAL LOSS IN THE EVENT OF DEFAULT. CREDIT RATINGS DO NOT ADDRESS ANY OTHER RISK, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO: LIQUIDITY RISK, MARKET VALUE RISK, OR PRICE VOLATILITY. CREDIT RATINGS ARE NOT STATEMENTS OF CURRENT OR HISTORICAL FACT. CREDIT RATINGS DO NOT CONSTITUTE INVESTMENT OR FINANCIAL ADVICE, AND CREDIT RATINGS ARE NOT RECOMMENDATIONS TO PURCHASE, SELL, OR HOLD PARTICULAR SECURITIES. CREDIT RATINGS DO NOT COMMENT ON THE SUITABILITY OF AN INVESTMENT FOR ANY PARTICULAR INVESTOR. MIS ISSUES ITS CREDIT RATINGS WITH THE EXPECTATION AND UNDERSTANDING THAT EACH INVESTOR WILL MAKE ITS OWN STUDY AND EVALUATION OF EACH SECURITY THAT IS UNDER CONSIDERATION FOR PURCHASE, HOLDING, OR SALE.\par \par \uc2 Copyright 2009, Moody's Investors Service, Inc. and/or its licensors and affiliates including \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Assurance Company, Inc. (together, "MOODY'S"). All rights reserved.\par \par \uc2 ALL INFORMATION CONTAINED HEREIN IS PROTECTED BY COPYRIGHT LAW AND NONE OF SUCH INFORMATION MAY BE COPIED OR OTHERWISE REPRODUCED, REPACKAGED, FURT