\par\par \b \uc2 DAVOS-INTERVIEW-IMF's Krueger-No need for  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  now.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Stella Dawson  \par \uc2 325 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2004\par 17:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 DAVOS, \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Jan 24 (Reuters) - A top policymaker at the International Monetary Fund said on Saturday that neither \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  nor interest rate cuts were warranted with the euro at current levels against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 In an interview with Reuters, IMF first deputy managing director Anne Krueger said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should be left to markets and financial policymakers should only consider action "in the most extreme circumstances".  \par \par \uc2 At current euro/dollar rates, she said "there is a strong presumption against" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "I know of no evidence as yet that this is the most extreme of circumstances - or even close. I emphasise 'as yet'," Krueger told Reuters on the sidelines of the annual World Economic Forum meeting.  \par \par \uc2 The euro has shot up 11 percent against the dollar in the past four months and has hit record highs, raising concern \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength will strangle the building euro zone recovery.  \par \par \uc2 European finance ministers are seeking support from their Group of Seven colleagues at an early February meeting in Florida for calming the rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves - whether through a G7 statement stressing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability is desirable or a commitment to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed.  \par \par \uc2 Krueger noted the euro/dollar real effective exchange rate is not far from its 30-year average. When asked whether a euro/dollar exchange rate of $1.30 would change her view, Krueger said: "No."  \par \par \uc2 "I know of no reason at this stage that we should be concerned or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in what seems appropriate in terms of the individual macroeconomic circumstances," Krueger said.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts are speculating that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be forced to cut interest rates to calm the euro's rise and ensure it does not undermine economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Krueger said the ECB should base its interest-rate decisions on what is best for the euro-zone economy and "not be constrained internationally," she said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040124e01o00a6p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
