\par\par \b \uc2 Swiss upturn prospects seen balancing soft dollar.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Armitage   \par \uc2 654 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2004\par 16:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 ZURICH, Jan 13 (Reuters) - A euro zone recovery and the relative weakness of the Swiss franc against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will boost Switzerland's economy and offset the impact of the soft dollar as it pulls out of a recession, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 However, whether the Swiss National Bank(SNB)would take part in any future concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the dollar remained uncertain, economists said, as the problem was seen as one of greenback weakness rather than franc strength.   \par \par \uc2 Top European bankers have expressed concern about the abrupt rise in the euro, while euro zone politicians called for ECB action to bring the euro-dollar rate back into line.   \par \par \uc2 The SNB also believes the weak dollar could harm the Swiss economy.   \par \par \uc2 "It puts an additional burden on economic development because we have companies which export largely to the dollar area," SNB spokesman Werner Abegg said.   \par \par \uc2 "But on the other hand we have seen quite a stable euro and a marked appreciation of the euro against the Swiss franc so at least there are counterbalancing elements there," Abegg said.   \par \par \uc2 Having fallen two percent since the start of the year, the dollar was last trading around 1.2270 francs on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 Bumping along at seven-year lows against the franc, the weak dollar makes life harder for firms with a high exposure to the U.S. and Asian nations whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are tied to the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Lombard Odier analysts warned that "as Swiss chemicals companies are all net exporters of goods, the competitive advantage they held for five entire years is seriously eroding."   \par \par \uc2 Switzerland's famous watchmaking industry has also been hit hard by the dollar's decline during a disappointing 2003, since around two-thirds of its exports go to the dollar area.   \par \par \uc2 Swiss Watch Industry Federation President Jean Daniel Pasche said the dollar weakness had made life tough for exporters. "But we are more confident for 2004 because we see a recovery in the U.S., Hong Kong and Japan," he said.   \par \par \uc2 SOFTENING SWISSIE   \par \par \uc2 Faced with a supercharged franc, the SNB slashed interest rates to near-zero levels on March 6 and took the wind out of the safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sails ahead of the war in Iraq.   \par \par \uc2 Some economists said at the time that the SNB had spent its ammunition too soon should the franc rise strongly again.   \par \par \uc2 However, the SNB's early move appears to have paid off with the franc falling steadily under what the export industry's pain barrier of 1.5000 level against the euro and helping stimulate a recovery driven by trade with major partners such as Germany.   \par \par \uc2 In a reverse of the watch industry's trade ratio, two-thirds of Swiss exports go to the euro zone while the remainder is destined for the United States or Asian nations.   \par \par \uc2 "An export-led recovery is picking up in Europe so the demand factor is compensating for any impediment from a weaker dollar," said Julius Baer Chief Economist Janwillem Acket.   \par \par \uc2 Acket added that since Switzerland had been able to keep the franc in check against the euro, it is not certain to join any future attempts to support the dollar through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "It is really the Europeans in the corner and \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been able to decouple a little thanks to the fact that the franc is weaker against the euro," Acket said.   \par \par \uc2 While the SNB has repeatedly stressed it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stave off a unwanted rise in the franc against the euro, traders note that it last took part in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1995 in a concerted move to prop up the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "They feel too small to do it," Huenerwadel said. "The SNB would be increasingly worried if the U.S. economy deteriorates going forward but so long as it is stampeding ahead they are not going to take decisive action."    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040113e01d003b7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
