\par\par \b \uc2  SNB's Roth says would  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  if franc spikes up. \b0\par\par\uc2 444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2003\par 11:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  FRIBOURG, \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 , June 26 (Reuters) - The Swiss National Bank would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if the Swiss franc spikes higher and threatens to hamper a recovery in the Swiss economy, SNB Chairman Jean-Pierre Roth said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Roth reiterated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would maintain its current expansionary monetary policy stance as long as inflation was low, adding that the SNB saw no risk of a protracted period of deflation for Switzerland. \par \par \uc2  "Deflation associated with a significant and lasting contraction in demand and production seems unlikely, as long as the Swiss franc does not rise strongly," he said in the text of a speech to the Swiss Federation of Watchmakers' annual meeting. \par \par \uc2  "In this case, we would take the necessary measures to reestablish the appropriate monetary conditions, including directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," he added. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said that with interest rates already near zero, they would be ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to quell any sharp rise in the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  Exports count for some 45 percent of Switzerland's gross domestic product and key industry groups, such as watchmakers, have railed against the safe-haven highs of the franc before the war in Iraq, which made their goods more expensive abroad. \par \par \uc2  Roth said that exchange rate developments were satisfactory overall. The franc has recently traded around 2-1/2 year lows against the euro, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its main trading partners. \par \par \uc2  "The recent correction in the franc versus the euro has caused an additional - and welcome - easing of monetary conditions," he said. "The course of our policy is therefore well adapted to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currenct\b0 \highlight\uc2  circumstances." \par \par \uc2  However, he added the SNB was aware of the problems which the recent weakness in the dollar caused for Swiss exporters. \par \par \uc2  "The fall in the rate of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  penalises firms which export to the dollar zone, particularly watch exports," he said. "The National Bank is well aware of the problems which the recent correction in the dollar could pose for our economy." \par \par \uc2  However, Roth said the trend in the dollar was a correction which reflects the readjustment of international capital movements: "In this sense, it is a problem of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than an appreciation of the Swiss franc." \par \par \uc2  The SNB has reiterated that cutting rates to the current target band of 0.0-0.75 percent had created the right conditions for Switzerland to benefit from a long-awaited upturn in the global economy. \par \par \uc2  "Monetary policy (in Switzerland) is currently expansive and it will remain so as long as price stability is not threatened," Roth said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030626dz6q00hfw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
