\par\par \b \uc2  Swiss SNB May Cut Rates,  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  If Franc Too Strong \b0\par\par\uc2 421 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2003\par 19:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  ST. GALLEN, \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- The Swiss National Bank said Friday it is prepared to cut rates further and to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the franc's strength were to pose a risk to growth. \par \par \uc2  "With money market rates below 0.75%, there isn't much room to maneuver, but there is still some," SNB Vice President Bruno Gehrig said in a speech to students at the university of St. Gallen, although he added that steering the franc reliably through interest rates was impossible. \par \par \uc2  The SNB, which targets the middle of a 0.25%-1.25% range for three-month Swiss franc Libor, or effectively 0.75%, has made six consecutive rate cuts in less than two years. \par \par \uc2  Libor has recently moved down to around 0.6% but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allows for fluctuations within the band without taking rate action. \par \par \uc2  "In addition, if necessary, we would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to correct exchange-rate fluctuations (strengthening of the Swiss franc) and provide the economy with more liquidity," Gehrig also said. \par \par \uc2  The SNB has often lowered rates when the franc strengthened beyond what is considered a pain threshold of CHF1.45 against the euro. Exporters and unions put that ceiling at CHF1.50. \par \par \uc2  The euro zone takes almost two-thirds of Swiss exports. \par \par \uc2  On the economy, Gehrig said he expects growth barely above 1% this year and inflation between 0.5% and 1%. Deflation was unlikely, Gehrig added. \par \par \uc2  "A comeback to long-term trend economic growth of around 1.5% to 2% will take a couple of quarters," he said, because of slow recovery elsewhere in Europe, especially Germany. The SNB views 2% growth as sustainable and non-inflationary. \par \par \uc2  Gehrig said the main risks to its outlook were political and military events that could both lead to a strong appreciation of the franc - because of its safe-haven status - and endanger economic stability. \par \par \uc2  The franc, which has strengthened to CHF1.4667 against the euro recently because of increasing fears of a war in Iraq, was at a record CHF1.4441 versus the euro last July, when the bank last cut rates by a generous half point. \par \par \uc2  Gehrig, whose resignation from the SNB's board takes effect in mid-2003, is credited with being the driving force behind the SNB's bias toward loose monetary policy in the past few years. \par \par \uc2  -By Antonio Ligi, Dow Jones Newswires; +41 1 212 2181; antonio.ligi@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030124dz1o0015q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
