\par\par \b \uc2  POLL-Swiss  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  to cap further franc gains. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Pratima Desai \par \uc2 929 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2002\par 14:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 24 (Reuters) - The threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will prevent the Swiss franc rising any higher against the dollar over the next year, even if war does break out in the Middle East, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said in a Reuters survey. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, the Swiss franc will come under pressure as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets also focus on Switzerland's dismal growth prospects compared to the slightly better ones in the euro zone. \par \par \uc2  In the poll of 45 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists taken October 7-10 the mid-range of forecasts put the euro at 1.4987 Swiss francs by the end of this year, at 1.48 by end-March 2003 and at 1.4924 by end-September from around 1.4675 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Until the end of next September one dollar is expected to buy about 1.48 Swiss francs, from around 1.5050 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "The Swiss franc is caught between two cross-currents," said John Normand at JP Morgan in London. "As long as there are fears of war...the risk aversion factor will be a positive. \par \par \uc2  "But the Swiss National Bank will resist further Swiss franc appreciation, either through interest rate cuts or through outright \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Ever since equity markets started tumbling in 2000 the Swiss franc has been climbing as investors trying to dodge the carnage by parking their money in Switzerland. \par \par \uc2  After the September 11 attacks the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced even higher as more money fled to the sanctuary of the Swiss franc on heightened geopolitical risks. \par \par \uc2  WHAT THE EXPERTS SAY \par \par \uc2  War rhetoric against Iraq by U.S. President George Bush has added to the uncertainty and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists put the chances of war between the United States and Iraq within the next six months at 70 percent. The average length of any conflict is put at around six weeks. \par \par \uc2  Eight expect it to start by end-2002, 27 said January or February, one said first quarter 2003, one said March, one said June and one thought anytime between November and January. \par \par \uc2  In a Reuters survey of 22 defence and Middle East experts, 13 said a U.S. invasion of Iraq with United Nations backing was likely or very likely within the next six months. Most expected the conflict would start in January or February and last no more than three months. \par \par \uc2  The timing of any invasion tallies with when strategists expect the Swiss franc to peak briefly against the dollar at 1.45, a level last seen in July. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar will come under a bit of pressure in January when we think an invasion could start," said Ray Attrill at 4CAST in London. "We don't think it will take too long...but the Swiss franc will become a safe-haven again for a while." \par \par \uc2  Any Swiss franc gains against the euro are expected to stop short of the 1.44 "pain level" for the economy, specified by SNB board member Niklaus Blattner last February. \par \par \uc2  Some think the euro's decline against the franc is unlikely to exceed 1.4450 - a level last seen in September 2001 - where traders have orders to take profits. \par \par \uc2  The euro zone's modest growth advantage and euro interest rates at 3.25 percent versus the less then one percent for the Swiss franc should support the euro, strategists said. \par \par \uc2  STAGNATION \par \par \uc2  Forecasts for the euro by the end of March next year ranged between 1.2430 and 1.7444 francs. \par \par \uc2  Michael Lewis at Deutsche Bank in London said any euro advantages will be eroded by Switzerland's persistent current account surplus which in uncertain times is a big attraction. \par \par \uc2  "Despite explicit \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rhetoric warning that the SNB will contemplate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...we would recommend selling euro/Swiss franc on any rallies," he added. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has in the past used \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to suppress franc gains and in the 1970s introduced a tax on foreign deposits to stop capital inflows. \par \par \uc2  This time, so far, they have halved two-week money market interest rates from above one percent in May. \par \par \uc2  Dollar forecasts for end-March 2003 ranged between 1.33 and 1.59 Swiss francs. Advocates of a stronger dollar cite Switzerland's stagnating economy. \par \par \uc2  In the first and second quarters of this year gross domestic product contracted by 0.4 and 0.7 percent year-on-year. \par \par \uc2  "It's likely Switzerland will have zero growth this year," said Tony Norfield at ABN Amro in London. \par \par \uc2  "It's not a determining factor, but the franc is one of the few world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with interest rates lower than the dollar." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Federal Reserve has cut benchmark interest rates to 40-year lows of 1.75 percent from 6.5 percent in January 2001. \par \par \uc2  Growth in the United States has slowed, but is nowhere near stagnation and the possibility of another cut in the funds rate is more likely to underpin the dollar then weigh on it. \par \par \uc2  Signs that equity markets are stabilising, perhaps even recovering, after the one of the longest bear runs in recent history are also a plus for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss authorities may try to prevent another surge in the franc's exchange rate against the euro and the dollar, but some believe the market will win in the end. \par \par \uc2  "The good chance of action in Iraq over the next few months and Middle East tensions mean global risk aversion is still rising," said David Mann at Standard Chartered Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  (Additional reporting by Ruth Pitchford and Hament Bulsara). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021024dyao00opj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
