\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Swiss won't exclude  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  to dampen franc. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Armitage \par \uc2 761 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2002\par 18:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  FEUSISBERG, \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Sept 26 (Reuters) - The Swiss National Bank will act quickly and decisively - perhaps even resorting to "extraordinary measures" such as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - should international jitters drive the Swiss franc higher, SNB Vice Chairman Bruno Gehrig said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "If some kind of shock causes a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement that would seriously damage our economy, then we would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he told reporters after a speaking engagement. \par \par \uc2  The remarks from Gehrig, well respected by financial markets for his experience as a commercial banker, were the clearest sign yet that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to go to almost any lengths to prevent a too-strong franc from crushing growth in the still fragile export-dependent economy. \par \par \uc2  "This is a measure of last resort, we have never used it in the recent past, but it is certainly a possibility which we would never exclude in principle," Gehrig added. \par \par \uc2  Addressing a business audience earlier on Thursday, Gehrig acknowledged that many people - mainly exporters and their suppliers - worried about the franc's exchange rate, especially against the euro, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Switzerland's main trading partners. \par \par \uc2  At times of increased uncertainty, money tends to flow into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of countries like Switzerland with large current account surpluses, he said. \par \par \uc2  "Unfortunately one cannot rule out the possibility that political/military events could trigger fresh fears and further strengthen upward pressure on the franc. But it is also possible that the development normalises," he said. \par \par \uc2  "If extraordinary shocks subject our stability to serious dangers, we will react decisively, if necessary with extraordinary measures as well," he said. \par \par \uc2  The National Bank rarely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly to buy or sell \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and then normally only as part of concerted action by other central banks. \par \par \uc2  Three decades ago, Switzerland went so far as to introduce negative interest rates on foreigners' Swiss franc deposits as a way to choke off an unwanted influx of funds and relieve upward pressure on the franc. \par \par \uc2  But the sweeping changes on financial markets in the meantime make a repeat of such stringent capital controls unlikely in this day and age, SNB officials say. \par \par \uc2  AGGRESSIVE CUTS \par \par \uc2  Aggressive rate cuts by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have dropped its benchmark target range for three-month interest rates by 2.25 percentage points over 2-1/2 years to just 0.75-1.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  Gehrig acknowledged that central banks are unable to steer a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange rate directly by raising or lowering interest rates, but noted easier monetary policy can help offset the restrictive impact of a rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The SNB has done that repeatedly over the past 18 months, he said, so even though the euro has moved in a rough range of around 1.45 to 1.48 francs since the September 11 attacks, the spread between Swiss and euro rates has widened to around 2.5 points from one point. \par \par \uc2  The SNB left rates steady a week ago at its quarterly policy review, but stressed its generous policy would continue until there was clear evidence that the sluggish economy was gaining momentum and pressure was easing on the safe-haven franc. \par \par \uc2  The economy expanded at a seasonally adjusted rate of just 0.4 percent in the second quarter versus the first and contracted 0.4 percent from the year-earlier period. \par \par \uc2  Gehrig said the SNB would not make the mistake of raising rates too early. \par \par \uc2  "Despite very low interest rates, no danger of inflation is perceptible for the foreseeable future. Even if the economic rebound turns out to be a positive surprise in the months and quarters ahead, there is enough time to make the necessary adjustments to interest rates," he said. \par \par \uc2  At the moment, however, "all indications now are that development in the second half of the year will remain weak. Overall growth this year should be just above zero percent," he said, and only a slow pickup was shaping up for 2003. \par \par \uc2  Gehrig told reporters later the Swiss economy could be growing at a rate of 1.5 to 2.0 percent toward the end of 2003. \par \par \uc2  Risks to the economy have shifted to the downside in recent months for the United States and even more so in Europe, he said, but he played down the prospects for Japanese-style deflation in Switzerland. \par \par \uc2  "Although inflation rates are low in Switzerland, we view deflation as very unlikely." Gehrig said, noting core inflation that strips out volatile items has been around one percent and domestic prices have been rising. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020926dy9q00wkm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
