\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland \highlight\uc2 /Lusser -2: Not Obligated To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 320 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1996\par 18:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  ZURICH (AP-Dow Jones)--Outgoing Swiss National Bank President Markus Lusser said Tuesday that the Deutsche mark-Swiss franc cross rate is 'decidedly stable.' \par \par \uc2  Lusser, in a speech set for delivery in Freiburg, Germany, noted that Switzerland isn't a member of the European Monetary System. 'The Swiss National Bank explicitly follows no \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate target,' he told a business audience. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  'is therefore also not obligated to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate reaches a certain level. \par \par \uc2  'Despite this, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate of the Swiss franc to the German mark, which has an especially important meaning to the Swiss economy, is decidedly stable,' Lusser said. \par \par \uc2  He traced this stability to the monetary policies of Germany and Switzerland that aim to keep inflation at bay. Lusser said both central banks are the only ones in the world that follow monetarist policies so strictly. \par \par \uc2  It came as no surprise, he said, that the more problematic \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were the weak European ones and the dollar. 'The countries with these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  don't follow a consistent monetarist strategy in the way the Deutsche Bundesbank and the Swiss National Bank do,' he said. \par \par \uc2  He also said that Switzerland's medium-term monetary policy allows it to react flexibly in the short-term to shifts in money demand. \par \par \uc2  The resolute fight against inflation pursued by the German and Swiss central banks, he said, makes both countries 'safe havens' and gives them the advantage of low interest rates. \par \par \uc2  'Foreign investors are prepared to invest their assets with us, with relatively low yields,' he said. 'They assume that our \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in the case of a crisis, won't lose value.' \par \par \uc2  But he noted that the Swiss franc can be subjected to more severe overshooting phases than the Deutsche mark faces. 'This represents a greater problem for our country,' he said. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 19-03-96 \par \par \uc2  1701GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds3j02gdg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
